[Music]  hello youtube chess lovers my friends  this is gunjan here  welcome to my 50th episode of chess trap  series  in this episode i am going to show you a  wonderful  high profile trap in the queen's gambit  decline  from the white perspective the most  amazing part is the chess legend  tigran petrossian was the first victim  of this trap and not only him  time to time many strong players  has fallen into this  well the critical position arises after  the following  opening model d4  d5 c4 e6  knight to c3 knight to f6  and now we have the exchange variation  c catches d5 e captures d5  and bishop to g5 so the idea is very  obvious  white wants to capture this knight and  if black recapture with the queen then  we can lop it off the d5 pawn  so wisely enough the main line continue  with  bishop to e7 unpinning the knot  and white liberate his second bishop  with e3 okay  c6 consolidating the center  and before we move on the important  point i like to mention here that  many people doesn't know why this is  considered as a main line  as c6 is definitely not a forcing move  but to understand this let's say black  wants to play  an active chess so most probably he  wants to take out the light squad bishop  and bishop to f5 looks the obvious  response  but this is exactly theory is very  helpful  because after bishop captures f6 bishop  captures f6  and the move queen to b3 you can see  suddenly two of the black pawns are  hanging  and unfortunately for black he cannot  defend  both of them so now you can understand  the importance of the c6 move  let's roll it back for the few moves  and we have the c6 mainline move  on the board and accordingly  white plays queen to c2  not allowing the dark square bishop to  be developed  and in his near future he wants to play  bishop to d3  and completely shut down this knight to  e4 idea  by black  well obviously black knows this and  that's why  many strong players has continued the  move  knight to e4 attacking the g5 bishop  well as per the online database this  move  has been played in more than 180 games  where i'm afraid black has straight away  fallen into white trapping territory  what actually black was hoping is  exchange of some minor pieces for  example  bishop captures e7 queen captures e7  and now after knight captures e4 and the  move  d captures e4 you can see black  managed to exchange two minor pieces and  he has  space in the center which already give  black a great equality just within  the opening stage so that is what  black is hoping for  but unfortunately if white wants  he can get a completely winning  advantage  from here so before i move on guys  i like you to pause this video and find  out  that killer sequence which  gives wide a completely upper hand  are you ready let's get start  the first move is very obvious bishop  captures e7  now it's worth to consider king captures  e7  because after this move white clearly  get a winning advantage  with the following sequence knight  catches e4  d captures e4 and queen captures e4  not only winning the pawn but black king  is completely misplaced  so nope king captures e7 is right out of  the equation  obvious move here is queen captures e7  hoping for knight captures e4  but this is where exactly black get the  root shock  and i hope you indeed spot it right  yup knight captures d5  bam  what a move  first thing first this knight is  attacking the queen  and queen has to stay in protection of  the e4 knight  so for example if black foolishly play  here  moves like queen e6 then after knight to  c7  check he is going to lose his queen  and the second option queen to h4 is  not good either because this time around  white will get the rook in the corner  so considering all this factor here the  fourth response is  c captures d5 but after this  white successfully regained the peace  with a pawn advantage  queen capture check and you might think  okay i will block it with the queen  and in end game i can fight for the draw  well guess what the position get  more worse with the following lovely  move  bishop to b5 jack  if the king moves then queen captures b7  is a very strong reply  same goes for knight to d7 so the only  logical reply here is  knight to c6 after that  the killer sequence continue bishop  capture c6  pawn capture c6 and now wonderful  queen capture c6 which completely  misplace  all black pieces because you cannot  block with queen  as your rook on 8 hangs and after the  funny move  king to f8 and knight to e2  if you carefully look at the final  position  not only wide emerge with a two point  advantage  but blacking is completely misplaced and  with the accurate play this is  definitely  a completely winning position  that's it guys i hope you enjoy and  learn this  high-profile trap in the queen's gambit  declined  remember once you open and play the moon  knight to e4  give him the root shock with the  following sequence  bishop captures e7 queen captures e7  and now knight captures d5 and after  this  however black plays he get a completely  losing position  thank you for watching this video feel  free to like subscribe and comment  and i will meet you in my next episode  very soon  bye and take care

